{"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/ivortuning\/welltemperament\/neidhardtii"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"patch":0,"major":0},"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"static"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"var"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"neidhardtII"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:10IvorTuning15WellTemperamentV","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorTuning\/documentation\/IvorTuning\/WellTemperament","text":"WellTemperament"},{"kind":"text","text":" { "},{"kind":"keyword","text":"get"},{"kind":"text","text":" }"}],"platforms":["macOS"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"level":2,"anchor":"discussion","type":"heading","text":"Discussion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Three fifths — C→G, G→D, D→A — are each narrowed by ⅙ of the Pythagorean comma"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"(≈ 698.045 cents). Six fifths — A→E, B→F♯, F♯→C♯, C♯→A♭, B♭→F, F→C — are each"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"narrowed by 1\/12 of the Pythagorean comma (≈ 700.000 cents). The remaining three"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"fifths — E→B, A♭→E♭, E♭→B♭ — are pure. The circle closes exactly:"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"3×(1\/6) + 6×(1\/12) = 1 Pythagorean comma."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"From "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"Sectio Canonis Harmonici","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" (Königsberg, 1724). Neidhardt relabeled this"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"specification “Large City” in his 1732 revision."}]}],"kind":"content"}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/IvorTuning\/documentation\/IvorTuning","doc:\/\/IvorTuning\/documentation\/IvorTuning\/WellTemperament"]]},"kind":"symbol","sections":[],"identifier":